# Audited false positives for check_secret_leak. Format: <path>[:<line>].
# A `#` comment is stripped, so every entry carries its reason above it.
#
# Line-pinned on purpose, and the trade-off is deliberate: an entry that drifts
# off its line stops matching and the gate REDS. That is the safe direction for
# a secret gate — someone re-audits and moves the pin — whereas a path-only
# entry would silently allow a real secret pasted anywhere in the file.

# gate-coverage.yml carries mutation-canary recipes, and this gate matches on
# CONTENT, so a canary that proves the gate can still SEE a private key has to
# contain a key-shaped string. The recipe below is the `check_secret_leak
# --pack` canary: a PEM header planted at a shipped-and-gitignored path
# (`dist/**`, which `files[]` ships and `.gitignore:206` ignores) to prove the
# pack scope reaches what no git-backed mode can. It is not a credential — the
# body reads `zz-canary-not-a-real-key` — and it is never written anywhere
# except by `check_gate_coverage --canary`, which deletes it in a `finally`.
#
# Its sibling `check_pack_size`'s canary needs NO entry here, because that
# gate's credential class matches on the PATH (`\.pem$`) and its recipe
# therefore carries no key-shaped body at all.
# RE-DERIVED 2026-08-24 after a merge re-pinned this twice in one day:
# 301 (chained-clip branch) and 306 (main, after the deterministic-time
# roadmap added manifest rows above the recipe) both described a tree that
# no longer exists. The merged file puts the recipe at 307, and the number
# is DERIVED from it rather than taken from either side — picking a side is
# how a line pin silently stops covering the line it was written for. The
# body was re-read before the pin moved: byte-identical, still
# `zz-canary-not-a-real-key`, still the gate's own planted canary.
# RE-PINNED 306 on 2026-08-24 (drain/road-to-score-contract). The 307 above was
# already wrong ON MAIN: `grep -n 'BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY'` reads 306 at
# origin/main and 306 on the branch, so the previous re-derivation landed one
# line high and the entry has been covering nothing since. It stayed invisible
# because this gate is DIFF-SCOPED — nothing red until a change touches
# gate-coverage.yml, which is exactly the latent shape a line pin trades for
# safety. Working as designed, in the direction the header above calls safe.
# Body re-read before the pin moved, per this file's own contract: byte-identical,
# still `zz-canary-not-a-real-key-zz`, still the gate's own planted canary.
# RE-PINNED 382 ON THE MERGE, 2026-08-24. Two drain branches each moved this pin
# correctly for their own tree — 335 and 339 — and after the merge BOTH manifest
# rows exist, so NEITHER number describes the file any more. This is the merge
# hazard the 335 entry below predicted, arriving exactly as predicted, and the
# resolution is the one it named: re-derive from
# `grep -n 'BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY' src/config/gate-coverage.yml` on the MERGED
# tree, never by picking one of the two numbers. Body re-read before the pin
# moved, per this file's own contract: byte-identical, still
# `zz-canary-not-a-real-key-zz`, still the gate's own planted canary.
#
# Fifth move in one day. The two shapes that would end it are recorded in the
# entries below — canary recipes in per-gate files, or a content-anchored allow
# form (`path:@<literal>`) — and both remain decisions rather than fixes.
#
# Both branch paragraphs are kept below rather than collapsed: each records a real
# re-derivation of a real tree, and the pair is the evidence that a line pin on
# this file drifts per-branch and not only per-commit.
#
# RE-PINNED 335 on 2026-08-24 (drain/road-to-skill-estate-drawdown). The
# `check_skill_admissions` manifest row landed above the recipe and pushed it down
# 29 lines. Derived from `grep -n 'BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY' src/config/gate-coverage.yml`
# on this branch, not by adding the diff's own delta. Body re-read before the pin
# moved: byte-identical, still `zz-canary-not-a-real-key-zz`.
#
# MERGE HAZARD, stated because two drain branches hit it in one day: a sibling
# branch (drain/road-to-command-surface-legibility) adds a DIFFERENT manifest row
# and pins 339. Whichever merges second, this pin will name a line that no longer
# holds the PEM, and `secret-vcs-guard` will red on the merge rather than on
# either branch. The fix is the same one line, re-derived from the grep on the
# MERGED tree — never by picking one of the two numbers, which is how a pin lands
# one line high (this file records that happening twice already).
#
# WORTH NAMING RATHER THAN RE-PINNING A FIFTH TIME: a line pin on a recipe inside
# an append-heavy manifest drifts on almost every change to that manifest, and on
# every parallel change independently. The header calls the drift direction safe
# and it is -- the gate reds rather than allowing. Two shapes would end it, both
# decisions rather than fixes: move canary recipes out of the manifest into
# per-gate files so the pin sits in a file nobody appends to, or teach
# `readAllowFile` a content-anchored form (`path:@<literal>`) so the entry follows
# the body it was audited against. Neither is done here; a drain run re-pinning a
# security suppression is not the place to redesign one.
# RE-PINNED 339 on 2026-08-24 (drain/road-to-command-surface-legibility). The
# `check_command_examples` manifest row landed ABOVE this recipe and pushed it
# down 33 lines, so 306 stopped covering it and CI red on `secret-vcs-guard` --
# the third time in one day this pin has moved, and each time for the same
# structural reason: the recipe sits near the END of a file that grows from the
# top. Derived from `grep -n 'BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY' src/config/gate-coverage.yml`
# on the merged tree, not adjusted by the +33 the diff happened to add. Body
# re-read before moving it, per the contract above: byte-identical, still
# `zz-canary-not-a-real-key-zz`.
#
# WORTH NAMING RATHER THAN RE-PINNING A FOURTH TIME: a line pin on a recipe
# inside an append-heavy manifest drifts on almost every change to that manifest.
# The header calls the drift direction safe and it is -- the gate reds rather
# than allowing. But the cost is a re-pin per manifest row, paid by whoever
# happens to add the next gate. Two shapes would end it and both are decisions
# rather than fixes: move canary recipes out of the manifest into per-gate files
# so the pin sits in a file nobody appends to, or teach `readAllowFile` a
# content-anchored form (`path:@<literal>`) so the entry follows the body it was
# audited against. Neither is done here; a drain run re-pinning a security
# suppression is not the place to redesign one.
# RE-PINNED 443 on 2026-08-26 (drain/dr1, road-to-canonical-terms 2.3). The
# `lint_canonical_terms` manifest row landed ABOVE this recipe and pushed it down
# 8 lines, so 435 stopped covering it and CI red on `secret-vcs-guard` -- the
# FOURTH time, for the same structural reason the paragraph above predicted and
# named. Derived from `grep -n 'BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY' src/config/gate-coverage.yml`
# on this tree, not adjusted by the +8 the diff happened to add. Body re-read
# before moving the pin, per the contract above: byte-identical, still
# `zz-canary-not-a-real-key-zz`, still inside a `content: |` block whose `path:`
# is `dist/zz-canary-shipped-and-gitignored.pem`.
#
# The two shapes that would end the drift are still the two named above, and are
# still decisions rather than fixes. This re-pin does not attempt either.

# RE-PINNED 443 -> 460 on 2026-08-26, and this one arrived through a MERGE plus
# a header recount in the same change: two branches each appended a manifest row
# (neither of which moved this pin, because both were appended at EOF), and the
# merge-recount paragraph above the rows added 17 lines that did. The standing
# mitigation "append the row at the bottom" worked exactly as intended and
# covered only half the exposure, which is the second time that has been true.
# Derived from `grep -n 'BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY' src/config/gate-coverage.yml` on
# the merged tree, never from either branch's delta — per-branch arithmetic is
# right on each branch and wrong on the merge, which is the whole failure mode
# of a line pin. Body re-read before moving it, per the contract above:
# byte-identical, still `zz-canary-not-a-real-key-zz`, still inside a
# `content: |` block whose `path:` is `dist/zz-canary-shipped-and-gitignored.pem`.
# RE-PINNED 443 -> 456 on 2026-08-26, the fifth time, and this one was caused by
# HEADER PROSE rather than by a manifest row: adding `lint_eval_specs` also
# recounted the population block above the rows, which is 13 lines. The row
# itself was appended at EOF specifically so it would NOT move this pin, and it
# did not — the prose did. Recorded because "append the row at the bottom" has
# been the standing mitigation and it is only half the exposure.
# Derived from `grep -n 'BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY' src/config/gate-coverage.yml` on
# this tree, never by adding the diff's delta. Body re-read before moving the
# pin, per the contract above: byte-identical, still
# `zz-canary-not-a-real-key-zz`, still inside a `content: |` block whose `path:`
# is `dist/zz-canary-shipped-and-gitignored.pem`.
# RE-PINNED 443 -> 457 on 2026-08-26, the FIFTH time and the first caused by a
# MERGE rather than by an edit. Two branches each added one manifest row above
# this recipe -- `check_beta_review_markers` and `lint_canonical_terms` -- and
# neither diff alone moved the pin by 14. Derived from
# `grep -n 'BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY' src/config/gate-coverage.yml` on the merged
# tree, never by adding either side's delta: the whole failure mode of a line
# pin is that per-branch arithmetic is right on each branch and wrong on the
# merge. Body re-read before moving the pin, per the contract above:
# byte-identical, still `zz-canary-not-a-real-key-zz`, still inside a
# `content: |` block whose `path:` is
# `dist/zz-canary-shipped-and-gitignored.pem`.

# RE-PINNED to 485 on 2026-08-26. The merge left TWO pin lines (460 from this
# branch, 457 from main) because keeping both sides of a conflict is right for
# an append-only list and wrong for a line pin, which is not additive. The stale
# one is deleted and the survivor is recomputed on the merged tree:
# `grep -n 'BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY' src/config/gate-coverage.yml` returns 485.
# Body re-read before moving it: byte-identical, still
# `zz-canary-not-a-real-key-zz`, still inside a `content: |` block whose
# `path:` is `dist/zz-canary-shipped-and-gitignored.pem`.
# RE-PINNED to 471 on 2026-08-26, and the merge produced TWO pins rather than a
# conflict in the number: both branches appended their own re-pin paragraph plus
# their own pin line, and keeping both sides left 456 and 457 side by side. A
# line pin is not additive, so the stale one is deleted and the survivor is
# recomputed on the merged tree — `grep -n 'BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY'
# src/config/gate-coverage.yml` returns 471. Body re-read before moving it:
# byte-identical, still `zz-canary-not-a-real-key-zz`, still inside a
# `content: |` block whose `path:` is `dist/zz-canary-shipped-and-gitignored.pem`.
#
# This is the sixth re-pin and the third caused by a MERGE. The two structural
# fixes that would end it are still the two named at the top of this file and
# both are still decisions rather than edits.

# RE-PINNED to 481 on 2026-08-26 — the fourth re-pin caused by a MERGE and the
# second where keeping both sides left TWO pin lines (485 and 471, each correct
# for its own branch). A line pin is not additive: both stale lines are deleted
# and the survivor is recomputed on the merged tree, `grep -n 'BEGIN RSA PRIVATE
# KEY' src/config/gate-coverage.yml` returning 481. Body re-read before moving
# it: byte-identical, still `zz-canary-not-a-real-key-zz`, still inside a
# `content: |` block whose `path:` is `dist/zz-canary-shipped-and-gitignored.pem`.

# RE-PINNED to 487 on 2026-08-27 — the seventh re-pin, and the first caused by
# an ADDED GATE ROW rather than a merge. `lint_consolidation_lineage` was
# registered at the TOP of `gates:`, which pushed every later row down by six
# lines. Recomputed on this branch's tree: `grep -n 'BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY'
# src/config/gate-coverage.yml` returns 487. Body re-read before moving it —
# byte-identical, still `zz-canary-not-a-real-key-zz`, still inside a
# `content: |` block whose `path:` is `dist/zz-canary-shipped-and-gitignored.pem`.
#
# Recomputed rather than derived by adding this diff's line delta: a delta is a
# claim about what else moved, and the grep is a measurement.
# RE-DERIVED 2026-08-30: this branch adds metric-gate rows to
# `gate-coverage.yml` above the canary recipe, moving it 487 -> 509. The
# number is read off the file rather than offset from the old pin, for the
# same reason the 2026-08-24 re-pin gives: an arithmetic guess is what put
# the pin on a tree that no longer existed.
#
# MOVED AGAIN 509 -> 516 on 2026-08-30, and it is the same cause a third time:
# resolving a merge conflict in `gate-coverage.yml`'s header added a seven-line
# recount block above the canary recipe. Derived from
# `grep -n 'BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY' src/config/gate-coverage.yml`, which returns
# 516 on the merged tree. Body re-read before moving the pin — it is still the
# `dist/zz-canary-shipped-and-gitignored.pem` recipe whose content is
# `MIIEowIBAAKCAQEAzz-canary-not-a-real-key-zz`, a canary fixture and not a key.
#
# The pattern is now measured rather than suspected: this pin has drifted on
# every change that adds prose above the recipe, which is what a line pin costs
# and what this file's own contract already says it trades for — a drift reds
# the gate and someone re-audits, which is the behaviour working, not failing.
#
# MOVED AGAIN 516 -> 525 on 2026-08-31, fourth time, same cause: the change that
# adds `lint_promotion_paths` put a nine-line recount block above the recipe.
# Derived from `grep -n 'BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY' src/config/gate-coverage.yml`,
# which returns 525 on this tree — read off the file, not offset from 516. Body
# re-read before moving the pin: still the
# `dist/zz-canary-shipped-and-gitignored.pem` recipe whose content is
